From: Thorch contributors
Subject: [PATCH] input: qcom-haptics: drop the LRA boot calibration retries

The AYN Thor buzzes three times at every boot and the driver throws the
measurements away.

haptics_hw_init() reads the LRA's resonant period out of the SDAM
register at boot. That uncalibrated reading is compared against the
LRA spec window (185-215 Hz) and, when it falls outside, the driver
re-measures up to three times, each attempt a full-amplitude direct play
with a 150 ms settle. On the Thor:

	lra frequency detected at boot: 170 Hz
	frequency after [1] calibration: 184 Hz
	frequency after [2] calibration: 183 Hz
	frequency after [3] calibration: 183 Hz
	lra frequency is still out of range, forced set to 205 Hz, ret:0

The actuator converges on the first attempt and stays there, at 183 Hz,
2 Hz under the 185 Hz floor. So the loop always ran to exhaustion and
then fell through to the forced 205 Hz anyway. Three loud buzzes per
boot, no effect on the frequency the motor ends up running at.

The retry exists to recover from a bad SDAM boot reading, which is what
the workaround for b/339117433 is about. Here the boot reading is 170 Hz
and the real measurement is 183 Hz, both out of spec by the same margin,
so re-measuring cannot reach the window either. And because 183 Hz is
discarded for the forced 205 Hz regardless, the retries were pure cost.

Keep the boot read and the out-of-range check so the frequency ends up
exactly where it does today, at LRA_SPEC_DEFAULT_FREQUENCY. Dropping the
loop removes the buzzes without changing the drive frequency.

Note the boot check reads config.cl_t_lra_us, which only ever comes from
the hardware status registers, not from the "qcom,lra-period-us" DT
property. Pinning that property in the DT cannot move this check.

haptics_start_lra_calibrate() is still used by the sysfs calibration
path, so only the forward declaration's role as an up-call from
haptics_hw_init() goes away.

One side effect to be aware of: the retries also populated the cached
LRA impedance range through haptics_detect_lra_impedance(). Nothing in
this driver consumes those fields, and on the Thor they are never
written to SDAM, since that only happens on the measure_lra_impedance
branch and the Thor has no "qcom,rt-imp-detect" property. The single
consumer is the lra_impedance sysfs attribute, which will now report
-EINVAL instead of a cached range until someone writes 1 to
lra_calibration by hand. Gameplay is unaffected.

Fixes: 0008-arm64-dts-qcom-qcs8550-ayn-thor-correct-haptics-lra-erm-flag.patch
---
 drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c | 21 +++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c
+++ b/drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c
@@ -3432,7 +3432,6 @@
 static int haptics_hw_init(struct haptics_chip *chip)
 {
 	int rc;
-	int retry = 0;
 	u32 cl_f_lra;
 
 	rc = haptics_config_wa(chip);
@@ -3464,14 +3463,19 @@
 	// Workaround for b/339117433 since we can't reproduce it
 	cl_f_lra = USEC_PER_SEC / chip->config.cl_t_lra_us;
 	dev_info(chip->dev, "lra frequency detected at boot: %d Hz\n", cl_f_lra);
-	while ((cl_f_lra < LRA_SPEC_MIN_FREQUENCY || cl_f_lra > LRA_SPEC_MAX_FREQUENCY) &&
-				retry < 3) {
-		haptics_start_lra_calibrate(chip);
-		cl_f_lra = USEC_PER_SEC / chip->config.cl_t_lra_us;
-		retry++;
-		dev_info(chip->dev, "frequency after [%d] calibration: %d Hz\n", retry, cl_f_lra);
-	}
-
+/*
+	 * No calibration retry loop here. Upstream re-measures up to three
+	 * times when the SDAM boot reading falls outside the LRA spec
+	 * window, but on the AYN Thor the actuator settles at 183 Hz on
+	 * the first attempt and stays there for every subsequent one.
+	 * That is 2 Hz under the 185 Hz floor, so the loop always ran to
+	 * exhaustion and then fell through to the forced 205 Hz below.
+	 *
+	 * Each attempt is a full-amplitude direct play with a 150 ms
+	 * settle, so the retries were three loud buzzes at every boot for
+	 * no effect on the frequency the motor actually ran at. Only the
+	 * measurement matters, and the measurement is discarded here.
+	 */
 	if (cl_f_lra < LRA_SPEC_MIN_FREQUENCY ||
 			cl_f_lra > LRA_SPEC_MAX_FREQUENCY) {
 		// HACK: forced set the frequency
